insert into reviewed_preference_descriptions (description) values
  ('Advocated sustainable community grants, programs for women- and minority-owned businesses, incentives for young entrepreneurs, business incubators, and cultural centers in an August 1, 2024 interview.'),
  ('As Pembroke Select Board chair, led the board''s 2021 interviews of applicants for the town''s new Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Committee. She asked applicants whether they had signed a petition to disband the committee.'),
  ('As Reynoldsburg council president pro tempore, Baker signed an ordinance sharing the city''s diversity, equality, and inclusion officer with the school district.'),
  ('As a Hartnell College trustee, moved to adopt a resolution affirming the college''s commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion. It passed.'),
  ('As a member of the Higher Education Budget Subcommittee, sent the University of Florida a 21-question oversight letter. It concerned the university''s public condemnation of diversity, equity, and inclusion programs. It also asked about its institutional-neutrality policy.'),
  ('As president of AAUW of Idaho, wrote a guest opinion criticizing the State Board of Education''s ban on diversity, equity and inclusion programs at public colleges. She also objected to the closing of campus women''s centers.'),
  ('Asked Greensboro City Council to keep funding its minority- and women-owned business program while considering a disparity study.'),
  ('Authored AB 766 requiring state agencies under the Governor to write and report diversity, equity, and inclusion plans. It passed the Assembly 60-18, but the Governor vetoed it on October 13, 2025.'),
  ('Cast the sole no vote against reappointing Theresa Devine to the Ellensburg Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Commission; the reappointment carried 6-1.'),
  ('Championed creation of Smithfield''s Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Task Force after racist graffiti was reported in town; the council approved its founding resolution.'),
  ('Chris Mathis publicly opposed Arizona SB1694''s restrictions on public-sector diversity programs, describing the measure as viewpoint discrimination.'),
  ('Co-offered Boston’s ordinance creating a supplier-diversity program and reporting requirements to increase meaningful participation by minority- and women-owned businesses in city contracts.'),
  ('Co-sponsored Senate Bill 26. The proposal bars Kentucky school districts and the state education department from spending on diversity, equity, and inclusion programs, and closes the department''s diversity division.'),
  ('Co-sponsored a Jefferson City Council bill to remove diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility (DEIA) policies from city boards, departments, and commissions; the City Council defeated the bill.'),
  ('Co-sponsored a Tennessee law requiring the head of each public college or university to certify yearly that the school used no preference favoring a group to increase diversity, equity, or inclusion. The certifications go to the state comptroller of the treasury, the state''s financial watchdog, starting January 1, 2027, and the law lets the attorney general investigate. It took effect May 22, 2026 (Senate Bill 1713).'),
  ('Co-sponsored a college overhaul that became law, barring race- or sex-based preferences and diversity, equity, and inclusion offices at Arkansas public universities (House Bill 1512, Act 341).'),
  ('Co-sponsored the Arkansas ACCESS Act, a higher-education law barring public colleges from requiring diversity statements and changing scholarship, concurrent-credit, and accelerated-course rules (House Bill 1512, Act 341 of 2025).'),
  ('Cosponsored HB 1788 in 2026 to void contracts containing DEI provisions and allow taxpayer lawsuits over public entities’ contracts with such provisions. The Senate referred it for interim study on April 16, 2026.'),
  ('During the 2025 Wyoming General Session, the Honor Wyoming legislative scorecard reports that Stacy Jones voted yes on SF 103, Terminating and defunding diversity, equity and inclusion.'),
  ('Introduced a bill on February 26, 2025 to raise the share of state contracts set aside for minority- and women-owned businesses from 15 to 20 percent. It did not pass.'),
  ('Introduced a bill with Rep. Anna Paulina Luna to ban race-based preferences and quotas in federal contracting.'),
  ('Introduced the Diversity, Equity, Inclusion Dead on Arrival Act.'),
  ('Issued a formal legal opinion that employer diversity, equity, and inclusion programs are legal under federal law.'),
  ('Issued a legal opinion asserting that roughly 80 Florida laws and programs supporting minority contracting and diverse appointments violated equal-protection guarantees, and said his office would no longer enforce or defend those provisions.'),
  ('Johnson sponsored enacted SB 1083 and SB 1084, known as the Dismantle DEI in Employment Act and Dismantling DEI Departments Act.'),
  ('Johnson sponsored enacted SB 1713 requiring annual attestations that public higher-education institutions have not implemented discriminatory preferences to increase diversity, equity, or inclusion and authorizing related attorney-general investigations.'),
  ('Joined a May 9, 2022 Supreme Court brief opposing race-conscious admissions at Harvard and the University of North Carolina. It sought reversal and overruling of Grutter under equal-protection and federal funding laws. It preserved a limited exception for remedying an institution''s own past discrimination.'),
  ('Joined a congressional brief opposing race-based college admissions in the Harvard and UNC cases. It argued that the policies discriminated and violated equal-treatment protections (Students for Fair Admissions).'),
  ('Joined the No Kings protest at the Michigan Capitol and opposed the Trump administration''s restrictions on diversity, equity, and inclusion programs.'),
  ('Joined the congressional amicus brief in the consolidated Harvard and UNC admissions cases arguing that race-conscious admissions violated equal protection and Title VI and urging the Supreme Court to overrule Grutter.'),
  ('Led a protest outside the U.S. Supreme Court in support of affirmative action during arguments in Schuette v. Coalition to Defend Affirmative Action.'),
  ('Made the motion to adopt San Mateo''s first citywide diversity, equity and inclusion plan for city operations; it passed 4-0.'),
  ('Made the motion to keep the city''s Diversity, Equity and Inclusion manager job. It failed 2-3.'),
  ('Made the motion to name affirmative action officers and require yearly equity training for all school staff. The board approved it 6-2.'),
  ('Made the motion to suspend the school division''s diversity, equity, and inclusion policy. It passed 4-3.'),
  ('Met his state representative as a constituent to argue against Alabama''s anti-DEI law, which limits diversity, equity, and inclusion programs (Senate Bill 129).'),
  ('Moved and voted to extend the district’s remedial program for minority- and women-owned business participation in construction, goods, and services.'),
  ('Moved to approve an agreement with Corwin for access, diversity, equity and inclusion services and products; the Governing Board approved it 3-0 on September 5, 2023.'),
  ('Moved to approve two contracts totaling $74,770 for diversity, equity, and inclusion training for city staff. The council approved them with no votes against.'),
  ('On 2024-06-26, voted to let large public-works bids be scored for participation by certified disadvantaged businesses, including minority-, women- and veteran-owned firms. The House passed the bill 39–0, and it became law.'),
  ('On 2025-01-13, Aleks Vranicic addressed the Mesa City Council and requested review of its diversity, equity and inclusion policy while suggesting merit-based decisions.'),
  ('On 2025-02-03, Aleks Vranicic addressed the Mesa City Council and requested that it reconsider and remove its diversity, equity and inclusion policy.'),
  ('On 2025-02-25, Josie Barnhart voted with the New Hanover County Board of Education majority to remove diversity, equity, and inclusion references from district policy; the vote was 5-2.'),
  ('On April 4, 2025, Representative Michelle McCane publicly condemned a U.S. Department of Education threat to withhold school funding unless schools certified bans on diversity, equity, and inclusion programs.'),
  ('On the Solano Community College board, seconded and voted for a resolution backing Proposition 16, which sought to end California''s ban on affirmative action. The same night, she voted for a resolution backing Proposition 15, higher property taxes on large commercial property to fund schools and local services. Both passed unanimously.'),
  ('Opposed Senate Bill 474 and defended diversity, equity, and inclusion, urging lawmakers not to support legislation driven by political hostility to DEI.'),
  ('Publicly defended the military''s diversity, equity and inclusion programs in a July 2023 interview, calling Republican attacks on them "a bunch of hooey."'),
  ('Seconded and voted for creating a school district inclusion and diversity officer job, which passed 5-2. He also moved a $100 bonus for staff who got a COVID-19 booster; it passed 4-3.'),
  ('Served as co-chair of the Minnesota Supreme Court Committee for Equality and Justice and led development of a judiciary diversity, equity, and inclusion training requirement.'),
  ('Signed Senate Bill 17 in June 2023, banning diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) offices and programs at Texas public colleges and universities.'),
  ('Sponsored Arizona House Concurrent Resolution 2042, which passed the House and proposed prohibitions on preferential treatment and discrimination based on race or ethnicity.'),
  ('Sponsored Maryland SB 829, extending and adding reports for the Minority Business Enterprise Program; the governor approved it as Chapter 621 on May 20, 2025.'),
  ('Sponsored Senate File 0103, titled Terminating and defunding diversity, equity and inclusion.'),
  ('Sponsored a bill proposing to withhold state money from Arizona public colleges and universities that offer diversity, equity, and inclusion courses. Governor Katie Hobbs vetoed it on May 5, 2025 (Senate Bill 1694).'),
  ('Sponsored a bill that barred waiving the state''s minority business contracting rules, even during a declared emergency. It passed the House 60-9 on July 1, 2021.'),
  ('Sponsored a law requiring the state to give contractors a list of certified minority- and women-owned businesses. It also makes the state publish every waiver of those hiring goals.'),
  ('Sponsored a law, signed on June 24, 2024, that lets the state''s chief purchasing officer waive contractor bond rules for certified minority- and women-owned businesses on public works jobs.'),
  ('Sponsored a law, signed on June 24, 2025, that requires the state to give every bidder on a construction project a list of certified minority- and women-owned businesses.'),
  ('Sponsored a law, signed on June 27, 2022, that requires the state diversity office to create yearly cultural competency training for all state and city workers.'),
  ('Submitted Phoenix budget comments supporting a diversity, equity and inclusion office and unarmed responders for noncriminal calls.'),
  ('Submitted testimony to the Defense Advisory Committee on Diversity and Inclusion opposing critical-race-theory and diversity, equity, and inclusion policies in the military.'),
  ('Testified for AAUW of Idaho against a bill banning diversity, equity and inclusion offices, diversity training and bias reporting systems at Idaho''s public colleges (Senate Bill 1198). She opposed it in Senate and House committees; it became law in April 2025.'),
  ('Voted against HB 2460, which restricted diversity, equity, and inclusion practices at public universities.'),
  ('Voted against House passage of SB129, the 2024 bill barring public universities and agencies from maintaining diversity, equity and inclusion offices and from teaching listed divisive concepts, which became Act 2024-34; the House vote was 75-28.'),
  ('Voted against keeping the city''s Diversity, Equity and Inclusion manager job. The motion to keep it failed 2-3.'),
  ('Voted against passing HB 1578, which extends Maryland''s minority business contracting program to July 2031. The Maryland House of Delegates passed it 98-32 and it was signed into law as Chapter 82 of 2026.'),
  ('Voted against passing S.B. 1, the Advance Ohio Higher Education Act, which banned diversity offices at public universities and barred faculty strikes. It passed the Ohio House 59-34 and was signed into law.'),
  ('Voted against suspending the school division''s diversity, equity, and inclusion policy. The suspension passed 4-3.'),
  ('Voted for ACA 5, proposing repeal of the state constitutional ban on race- and sex-conscious affirmative action in public education, employment and contracting. It passed the Assembly 60–14.'),
  ('Voted for BART Resolution 5446 supporting repeal of Proposition 209 and restoration of affirmative action in California; the board adopted the resolution unanimously.'),
  ('Voted for HB2105 (81-40), restricting diversity, equity, and inclusion practices at public colleges.'),
  ('Voted for House Bill 2868, a bill to ban diversity, equity and inclusion offices across much of Arizona government. The Arizona Senate passed it 17-11, and the Governor vetoed it.'),
  ('Voted for House Bill 4, which bars public colleges from spending on diversity, equity and inclusion or treating people differently by race or sex. The Kentucky House voted 79-19, and the bill became law over the Governor''s veto.'),
  ('Voted for House Bill 638, which bars state and local agencies from asking for a diversity statement. The Montana House passed it 57-41, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for House Bill 638, which bars state and local agencies from asking for a diversity statement. The Montana Senate passed it 31-19, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 2487, a bill to give the attorney general new power to enforce the ban on diversity offices. The Iowa House passed it 62-27, but the full Senate never voted on it, so it did not become law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 2488, a bill to bar private colleges whose students get state tuition grants from running diversity offices. The Iowa House passed it 57-34, but the full Senate never voted on it, so it did not become law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 2711, which removes affirmative action from Iowa law. The Iowa House passed it 64-29, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 2711, which removes affirmative action from Iowa law. The Iowa Senate passed it 31-15, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 856, which bars governments and public colleges from spending money on diversity, equity and inclusion programs. The Iowa House passed it 59-32, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for House File 856, which bars governments and public colleges from spending money on diversity, equity and inclusion programs. The Iowa Senate passed it 34-16, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1084, the Dismantling DEI Departments Act, which bars diversity offices and preferences in government and public colleges. The Tennessee House passed it 72-25, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1198, which bans diversity, equity and inclusion offices and required training at public colleges. The Idaho House passed it 52-17, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1198, which bans diversity, equity and inclusion offices and required training at public colleges. The Idaho Senate passed it 23-12, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1256, a bill to bar state agencies from using diversity, equity and inclusion programs in hiring. The Arizona House passed it 33-25, and the Governor vetoed it.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1256, a bill to bar state agencies from using diversity, equity and inclusion programs in hiring. The Arizona Senate passed it 17-12, and the Governor vetoed it.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1694, a bill to cut off state money to any public college that offers a diversity, equity and inclusion course. The Arizona House passed it 32-23, and the Governor vetoed it.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 1694, a bill to cut off state money to any public college that offers a diversity, equity and inclusion course. The Arizona Senate passed it 16-11, and the Governor vetoed it.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 289, which bars public employers and schools from using race or sex in hiring, admissions or aid. The Indiana House backed the final version 64-26, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 289, which bars public employers and schools from using race or sex in hiring, admissions or aid. The Indiana Senate backed the final version 34-16, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted for Senate Bill 295, which applies the ban on diversity, equity and inclusion programs to school clubs. The Utah House passed it 48-16 and it was signed into law.'),
  ('Voted for Substitute for HB 2460, restricting diversity, equity, and inclusion policies in public postsecondary admissions and employment.'),
  ('Voted for a council resolution reaffirming Lewiston''s commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion; it passed unanimously.'),
  ('Voted for a higher education overhaul that ended diversity programs at public colleges, barred faculty strikes, and added performance reviews for tenured faculty (Senate Bill 1).'),
  ('Voted no on House Bill 171, a bill to stop state agencies and local governments running diversity, equity and inclusion programs. The Senate voted 26-20 and the governor vetoed the bill. The House voted to override in 2026 but the Senate has not, so it is not law.'),
  ('Voted no on Senate Bill 227, which bars public schools from running diversity offices or teaching what the law calls divisive concepts. The Senate voted 30-19 to override in July 2025, the House followed in June 2026, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted no on Senate Bill 227. Public schools may no longer run a diversity, equity and inclusion office, or teach what the law calls divisive concepts. One banned idea is that one race or sex is better than another. Free speech and normal history teaching are protected. Each school must report every year that it follows the law. A no vote would have let the governor''s veto stand. The House voted 71-46 to override in June 2026, after the Senate did so in July 2025, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted no on Senate Bill 558, which bars public colleges from keeping diversity offices or teaching what the law calls divisive concepts. The Senate voted 30-19 to override in July 2025, the House followed in June 2026, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted no on Senate Bill 558. State universities and community colleges may no longer keep a diversity, equity and inclusion office, or employ staff to promote what the law calls divisive concepts. They also may not require a class on those ideas for a degree unless the chancellor agrees. They may not take reports about speech that the First Amendment protects. A no vote would have let the governor''s veto stand. The House voted 71-46 to override in June 2026, after the Senate did so in July 2025, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted no on adding the city Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Commission''s draft wording to Ellensburg''s long-range growth plan (Docket Item 25-02.001-.308). The council approved it 5-2, with Councilmember Delano Palmer also opposed.'),
  ('Voted no on disbanding Enfield''s Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Committee, saying the town should fix it instead. The council disbanded it 7-4.'),
  ('Voted no on naming affirmative action officers and requiring yearly equity training for all school staff. The board approved it 6-2.'),
  ('Voted to accept the House''s changes to Senate Bill 1, the Advance Ohio Higher Education Act, banning diversity offices at public universities and faculty strikes. The Ohio Senate agreed 20-11, and the governor signed it on March 28, 2025.'),
  ('Voted to extend the district’s minority- and women-owned business remedial participation program.'),
  ('Voted to pass H 3927, the Ending Illegal Discrimination and Restoring Merit-Based Opportunity Act, a bill to bar diversity offices in government and schools. The South Carolina House passed it 82-32. As of September 2026 the Senate had not voted on it, so it has not become law.'),
  ('Voted to pass HB 1578, which extends Maryland''s minority business contracting program to July 2031. The Maryland House of Delegates passed it 98-32 and it was signed into law as Chapter 82 of 2026.'),
  ('Voted to pass HB 622, which bars local governments from using race, sex or age in hiring decisions. The Tennessee House passed it 73-24, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted to pass HB 742, a bill to bar state departments from spending on diversity, equity and inclusion programs. The Missouri House passed it 108-50. The Senate did not vote on it, so it did not become law.'),
  ('Voted to pass House Bill 4, which bars public colleges from spending on diversity, equity and inclusion or treating people differently by race or sex. The Kentucky Senate voted 32-6, and the bill later became law over the Governor''s veto.'),
  ('Voted to pass S.B. 1, the Advance Ohio Higher Education Act, which banned diversity offices at public universities and barred faculty strikes. It passed the Ohio House 59-34 and was signed into law.'),
  ('Voted to pass SB 376, which bars four-year colleges from treating people differently by race, ending race-conscious admissions. The Tennessee House passed it 73-22, and it became law.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 129, which shut down diversity, equity and inclusion offices in state agencies, schools and public colleges. The House passed it 75-28 and it became law.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 129, which shut down diversity, equity and inclusion offices in state agencies, schools and public colleges. The Senate passed it 26-7 and it became law.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 3, which bars state and local government from treating anyone better or worse because of race, sex or ethnicity. The Arkansas House passed it 65-27 and it became Act 116 of 2025.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 3, which bars state and local government from treating anyone better or worse because of race, sex or ethnicity. The Arkansas Senate passed it 24-6 and it became Act 116 of 2025.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 520, which bars counties, cities and towns from running a diversity, equity and inclusion program. The Arkansas House passed it 68-22 and it became Act 747 of 2025.'),
  ('Voted to pass Senate Bill 520, which bars counties, cities and towns from running a diversity, equity and inclusion program. The Arkansas Senate passed it 22-7 and it became Act 747 of 2025.'),
  ('Voted with the full board, 7-0, to remove a 30-year district policy that encouraged hiring minority-owned and local businesses. The board waived the usual second reading.'),
  ('Voted yes on Alabama House passage of SB129, barring public universities and agencies from maintaining diversity, equity and inclusion offices; the measure passed 75-28 on March 7, 2024.'),
  ('Voted yes on House Bill 171, a bill to stop state agencies and local governments running diversity, equity and inclusion programs. The Senate voted 26-20 and the governor vetoed the bill. The House voted to override in 2026 but the Senate has not, so it is not law.'),
  ('Voted yes on House final passage of HI 1000, Initiative 1000, restoring affirmative action in state contracting, employment and public education, on 2019-04-28; the measure passed the House 56-42.'),
  ('Voted yes on House passage of SB 129 in the 2024 regular session. The law barred Alabama public universities, K-12 systems and state agencies from sponsoring diversity, equity and inclusion programs. It also barred teaching listed divisive concepts. It passed 75-28 on House Roll Call 222 and became Act 2024-34.'),
  ('Voted yes on Senate Bill 227, which bars public schools from running diversity offices or teaching what the law calls divisive concepts. The Senate voted 30-19 to override in July 2025, the House followed in June 2026, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted yes on Senate Bill 227. Public schools may no longer run a diversity, equity and inclusion office, or teach what the law calls divisive concepts. One banned idea is that one race or sex is better than another. Free speech and normal history teaching are protected. Each school must report every year that it follows the law. A yes vote backed making the bill law over the governor''s veto. The House voted 71-46 to override in June 2026, after the Senate did so in July 2025, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted yes on Senate Bill 558, which bars public colleges from keeping diversity offices or teaching what the law calls divisive concepts. The Senate voted 30-19 to override in July 2025, the House followed in June 2026, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted yes on Senate Bill 558. State universities and community colleges may no longer keep a diversity, equity and inclusion office, or employ staff to promote what the law calls divisive concepts. They also may not require a class on those ideas for a degree unless the chancellor agrees. They may not take reports about speech that the First Amendment protects. A yes vote backed making the bill law over the governor''s veto. The House voted 71-46 to override in June 2026, after the Senate did so in July 2025, and the bill became law.'),
  ('Voted yes on barring state universities from spending on diversity, equity and inclusion programs (SB 266); it passed the House 81-34.'),
  ('Voted yes on motion B/C 23-034, adopting a resolution that closed the Ottawa County Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion Department; the motion passed 7 to 1.'),
  ('Watson voted for HB 622 and SB 1084 in 2025, prohibiting specified diversity, equity, and inclusion offices, initiatives, and hiring practices in state and local government and public higher education.'),
  ('Wrote an op-ed arguing that diversity, equity and inclusion programs matter for their results, not their name.');
